Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog audit** of `OrderProcessorTests.cs`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 5           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | 8 (class-wide) |
| **Total**| **10**      | **8 of 9**     |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

---

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic

**Test:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)
**Evidence:** `if`/`else` block (lines 31–38) chooses between two different assertions at runtime. One branch is never verified per run.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Fix — two deterministic tests with controlled inputs:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardAmount_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumAmount_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is never exercised — hidden gap in coverage.

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Test:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)
**Evidence:** Calls `ProcessOrder` but has zero assertions (lines 44–48). Passes even if the order is silently corrupted.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — always passes unless an exception is thrown
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
```

**Fix — assert on the outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test inflates coverage but verifies nothing.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test

**Test:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)
**Evidence:** `Thread.Sleep(2000)` on line 98 introduces a hard 2-second wall-clock wait.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);
Thread.Sleep(2000);
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

**Fix — use a polling helper or make the fake awaitable:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
